{"causes": ["The flourishing of troubadour culture in Occitania during the 12th century provided a fertile ground for poetic innovation and patronage.", "The relative autonomy and cultural influence of noblewomen in Southern France allowed some to pursue artistic and literary endeavors.", "The development of the *fin'amor* tradition created a thematic framework for courtly poets, including women, to explore love and chivalry."], "effects": ["Azalaïs de Porcairagues's work contributed to the corpus of trobairitz poetry, offering a unique female perspective on courtly love.", "Her existence challenged later historical narratives that largely overlooked female literary contributions in the High Middle Ages.", "Her surviving *canso* serves as a primary source for understanding the sophisticated poetic forms and social dynamics of 12th-century Occitan courts."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "azalas-de-porcairagues", "sourceName": "Azalaïs de Porcairagues", "verb": "CREATES", "targetSlug": "no-sap-chantar-qui-so-non-di", "targetName": "'No sap chantar qui so non di'", "context": "Azalaïs de Porcairagues is credited with composing the *canso* 'No sap chantar qui so non di' in the late 12th century."}, {"sourceSlug": "raimbaut-daurenga", "sourceName": "Raimbaut d'Aurenga", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "azalas-de-porcairagues", "targetName": "Azalaïs de Porcairagues", "context": "Raimbaut d'Aurenga, a prominent troubadour, influenced Azalaïs de Porcairagues through his poetic style and their mutual engagement with courtly themes around 1170-1190."}, {"sourceSlug": "azalas-de-porcairagues", "sourceName": "Azalaïs de Porcairagues", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "languedoc-france", "targetName": "Languedoc, France", "context": "Azalaïs de Porcairagues lived and composed her poetry within the historical region of Languedoc, France, during the late 12th century."}, {"sourceSlug": "azalas-de-porcairagues", "sourceName": "Azalaïs de Porcairagues", "verb": "INFLUENCE