": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#38118047648", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Azalais de Porcairagues or Alasais de Porcaragues was a trobairitz, composing in Occitan in the late 12th century.", "newValue": "Azalaïs de Porcairagues, a prominent trobairitz flourishing in the late 12th century, stands as one of the earliest documented female poets in Western literary history, composing sophisticated courtly love lyrics in Old Occitan. Hailing from the noble house of Porcairagues in Languedoc, she was part of the vibrant cultural milieu that defined the High Middle Ages in southern France, a region renowned for its innovative poetic and musical traditions. Her existence challenges conventional narratives of medieval literary production, demonstrating female agency and intellectual prowess within a predominantly male-dominated artistic landscape, particularly through her notable connection to Raimbaut d'Aurenga.

Her poetic output, though limited in surviving quantity to a single *canso*, 'No sap chantar qui so non di', reveals a mastery of the complex metrical and rhetorical forms characteristic of troubadour poetry. This *canso* is celebrated for its intricate rhyme scheme and its subtle exploration of *fin'amor*, or courtly love, often interpreted as a dialogue or response to the works of her contemporary male troubadours. Azalaïs's engagement with these established themes, while injecting a distinct female perspective, underscores her active participation in and contribution to the intellectual discourse of her time, not merely as a patron but as a creative force.

The enduring legacy of Azalaïs de Porcairagues lies primarily in her symbolic importance as a voice for medieval women and as a testament to the rich, diverse literary traditions of Occitania. Her work, alongside that of other trobairitz, provides invaluable insight into the cultural and social dynamics of the medieval courts, illustrating how women navigated and influenced artistic expression. This body of work has profoundly shaped modern understanding of medieval gender roles, literary history, and the evolution of European lyric poetry, influencing later traditions of romantic verse."}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-11T06:29:44Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#38118047648", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1102, http://www.wikidata.org/.well-known/genid/aad7df1d37d59c049e1ecf86a32aeb4a"}, {"title": "Poetic traditions and dramatic performance arts", "type": "Idea", "year": "1102, http://www.wikidata.org/.well-known/genid/aad7df1d37d59c049e1ecf86a32aeb4a"}], "newValue": ["The flourishing of troubadour culture in Occitania during the 12th century provided a fertile ground for poetic innovation and patronage.", "The relative autonomy and cultural influence of noblewomen in Southern France allowed some to pursue artistic and literary endeavors.", "The development of the *fin'amor* tradition created a thematic framework for courtly poets, including women, to explore love and chivalry."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-11T06:29:44Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#38118047648", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Contributed poetry and drama to cultural canon", "type": "Idea", "year": "1102"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1102"}], "newValue": ["Azalaïs de Porcairagues's work contributed to t